# Can markets deliver any fair outcome we want?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What does the second welfare theorem promise? Why does it need lump-sum transfers that nobody can design?"
next: {"title":"How do we add up everyone's welfare?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/28-how-do-we-add-up-everyone-s-welfare.cast"}

## The promise
use: ppf
with:
    good_x: Ada's well-being
    good_y: Jon's well-being
    x_max: 10
    y_max: 10
    shade_attainable: true
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 60
      w: 660
      h: 600
    points:
      - {x: 1.6, 'y': 3.6, label: Start, kind: inefficient}
      - {x_share: 0.18, label: Market}
      - {x_share: 0.55, label: Fair}
      - {x: 4.2, 'y': 1.6, label: New start, kind: inefficient}

    arrow trade1 pt_0 -> pt_1 #3d3833 hidden true
    arrow transfer pt_0 -> pt_3 #b5482e hidden true
    arrow trade2 pt_3 -> pt_2 #2f6b8f hidden true
    label lumpsum "lump-sum" left #b5482e attach_to transfer hidden true
    icon jon at.data [4.6,11.7] size 70 or ["boat","sailboat"] icon_look picture of "fishing boat" hidden true
    icon ada at.data [11.8,2] size 70 or ["fish","tropical fish"] icon_look picture of "fishing pole" hidden true
    scratch thm work ["2nd welfare theorem","any efficient point =","transfer + market"] x 840 y 430 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Can markets deliver any fair outcome?"

Can a free market deliver any fair outcome we want, or only the one it happens to land on?
    draw axes

Take two fishers in Har (@draw pt_0 pt_label_0@)bourtown, with illustrative numbers. Jon is gif (@draw jon@)ted and owns a boat; Ada fish (@draw ada@)es from the pier.

Every point on this curve is efficient: nobody can gain without the o (@draw attainable@)ther losing. Jon's well-being goes up, Ada's goes across.
    draw frontier

B: Left alone, they trade from where they (@draw trade1@) start until no deal is left. That lands them here (@draw pt_1 pt_label_1@), on the curve, but far up Jon's end.

Efficient, yes. Fair? Most people would pi (@draw pt_2 pt_label_2@)ck somewhere more like this.

    quiz "Can a market take Harbourtown to the point Fair?" judge false voice b
        intro: So can a market get them there too? Your guess first.
        right: Yes, if the starting point moves first. Watch.
        * No, only to Market
        * Yes, if we first change where they start
        * Only if the council sets prices

    fade trade1 to 0.3

Here's the trick: before anyone trades, (@draw transfer pt_3 pt_label_3@) move part of Jon's catch to Ada. The starting point moves.

Then let them trade freely, at market prices, and t (@draw trade2@)he trades carry them out to the curve, right at the fair point.

B: So the market does the efficient part, and one transfer at the start does the fair part.
    highlight pt_2 effect circle

That's the second we (@draw thm_box thm_line_1@)lfare theorem, proved in its modern form by Kenneth Arrow and Gérard Debreu in the early 1950s. It nee (@draw thm_line_2@)ds the ideal market of the first welfare theorem, plus no big economies of scale.

B: And one line of small print: the transfer must be (@draw thm_line_3 lumpsum@) lump-sum, an amount nothing Jon does can change.

    pause 0.5

## Why lump-sum?
use: indifference_budget
with:
    income: 30
    price_x: 3
    price_y: 1
    preference: balanced
    curves: 1
    x_good: Hours off
    y_good: Crates kept
    x_max: 12
    y_max: 32
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 40
      w: 700
      h: 640

    scratch read work ["No tax: 5h off, 15 kept","Flat 6: 4h off, 12 kept","40%: 5h off, 9 kept"] x 815 y 430 font_size 23 hidden true
    text x10 "10 h" #8f887c at.data [10,-1.6] font_size 20 hidden true
    text x5 "5" #8f887c at.data [5,-1.6] font_size 20 hidden true
    text x4 "4" #8f887c at.data [4,-1.6] font_size 20 hidden true
    text y30 "30" #8f887c at.data [-0.45,30] font_size 20 hidden true
    text y15 "15" #8f887c at.data [-0.45,15] font_size 20 hidden true
    text y12 "12" #8f887c at.data [-0.45,12] font_size 20 hidden true
    text y9 "9" #8f887c at.data [-0.45,9] font_size 20 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch m work ["Flat 6: keep = 3h − 6","40%: keep = 0.6 × 3h = 1.8h","extra hour: 3 vs 1.8 crates"] x 815 y 215 font_size 22 hidden true

    card title "Why must it be lump-sum?"

B: One transfer at the start reaches any fair point. Why must it be lump-sum? Look at Jon's day.
    draw axes

B: Say he has ten hours. Every hour at s (@draw budget_line label_budget@)ea brings three crates, so this line is every mix of hours off and c (@draw x10 y30@)rates he can have.

B: His curv (@draw ic_1@)es show what he likes. He picks f (@draw optimum guide_x guide_y@)ive hours of (@draw x5 y15@)f, and  (@draw read_box read_line_1@)fifteen crates.

Now the council wants six crates from him, for Ada. The lump-sum way first?
    highlight budget_line

B: Six crates, whatever he does. The whole line slides down.
    animate income 24 duration 2.5 ghost true

    hide label_ic_1 optimum_label

B: He takes four hours  (@draw read_line_2 x4 y12@)off now and fishes six. An extra hour at sea still pays the full three crates.

And the other way: forty percent of every crate. That also raises six crates. My guess: same money, same fishing.
    highlight read_line_2

    quiz "Same six crates, taken as 40% of every crate. Will Jon fish more or less than under the flat six?" judge false voice b
        right: Less. Watch what the tax does to his line.
        * More
        * The same
        * Less

B: Less. The line turns down: an hour at sea now pays him only one point eight crates.
    animate price_x 1.8 income 18 duration 2.5 ghost true

    hide label_ic_1 optimum_label

B: So he fishes five ho (@draw read_line_3 y9@)urs, not six. The town lands three crates fewer, and Jon ends on a lower curve.

Same six crates for Ada, less fish for everyone. That lost catch is the cost of a tax you can dodge by working less.
    highlight read_line_2 read_line_3

B: Try it: slide the line down for a flat tax, or turn it at its end for a tax per crate, and watch how long Jon fishes.
    explore

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: There's a little algebra behind those two lines.

@math

    erase mathq

With the flat tax, Jon keeps (@draw m_box m_line_1@) three crates an hour minus six: each extra hour still pays three.

B: With forty percent, he keeps poi (@draw m_line_2@)nt six of three crates an hour, one point eight: each extra hour pays less.

Both raise six crates. Only the second ch (@draw m_line_3@)anges what an hour at sea is worth, and that's the distortion.

    erase m

@test

    erase mathq

So a lump-sum transfer moves crates without touching anyone's choices. That's why the theorem needs it.
    highlight read_line_2 effect underline

    pause 0.5

## Nobody can see talent
sources:
    - id: mirrlees1971
      title: An Exploration in the Theory of Optimum Income Taxation
      authors: Mirrlees, J. A.
      year: 1971
      finding: Optimal income tax when the government observes income but not the ability behind it; basis of the 1996 Nobel prize (with Vickrey) for incentives under asymmetric information.
    - id: polltax
      title: Poll tax (Great Britain)
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2024
      finding: The Community Charge, a flat per-adult local tax, began in Scotland in 1989 and England and Wales in 1990; protests in London on 31 March 1990 turned into riots; replaced by the Council Tax in 1993.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poll_tax_(Great_Britain)
    - id: mw2010
      title: 'The Optimal Taxation of Height: A Case Study of Utilitarian Income Redistribution'
      authors: Mankiw, N. G. & Weinzierl, M.
      year: 2010
      finding: Under the standard utilitarian framework a tall person earning $50,000 should pay about $4,500 more in tax than a short person; if that is rejected, the framework misses intuitive notions of justice.
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w14976

    box n_jon "Jon" icon "fishing boat" in fishers hidden true
    box n_ada "Ada" icon "fishing pole" in fishers hidden true
    row fishers gap 180 fit.h 300 fit.w 620 fit.x 60 fit.y 300 hidden true
    text t_jon "talent: 3 crates/h" below n_jon gap 24 #b5482e font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_ada "talent: 1 crate/h" below n_ada gap 24 #2f6b8f font_size 26 hidden true
    text q_jon "?" right n_jon gap 24 #b5482e font_size 48 hidden true
    mark strike around t_jon kind strike hidden true
    text sees "the council sees only the catch" x 370 y 200 hidden true
    portrait mirrlees cites ["mirrlees1971"] x 850 y 440 width 200 of "James Mirrlees" cameo true hidden true
    cards dodge select "Can't dodge" check each hidden true
        * "$500 per adult" in true
        * "Tax per crate" in false
        * "Income tax" in false
        * "Tax on height" in true
        * "Sales tax on bread" in false
        * "IQ test at 18" in false
    scratch poll cites ["polltax"] work ["Poll tax, Britain","flat charge per adult","1990 riots · gone 1993"] x 265 y 450 font_size 32 hidden true
    scratch height cites ["mw2010"] work ["Height tax?","tall, earning $50,000,","vs a short person:"] x 735 y 450 font_size 32 hidden true
    text big "+$4,500 a year" #b5482e cites ["mw2010"] x 720 y 270 font_size 56 hidden true

    card title "Nobody can see talent"

So a lump-sum transfer is the clean one. Why don't governments just use it?
    draw n_jon n_ada

B: Because to be fair it has to hit talent, and nobody can see talent. Say (@draw t_jon@) Jon catches three crates an hour,  (@draw t_ada@)and Ada one.

Then tax the talent. Three crates an hour pays more, whatever he chooses to do.
    highlight t_jon

B: And who tells the council it's three? Jon  (@draw strike q_jon@)can fish slowly on test day and look just like Ada.

So any tax Jon can change, he can dodge. And a tax he can't (@draw sees@) change needs facts that only Jon knows.

B: James Mi (@draw mirrlees@)rrlees made exactly that the starting point of tax theory, in 1971: the state sees income, never ability. It earned him a share of the 1996 Nobel prize.

    erase mirrlees n_jon n_ada t_jon t_ada q_jon strike sees

B: Your turn. Which of these could nobody dodge?
    draw dodge

    ask "Which of these taxes could nobody dodge, whatever they do? Tap every one." on dodge store f voice b
        right: Right: only the flat charge per adult and height. You can work less, buy less, or score low on a test on purpose.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Only the flat charge per adult and height can't be dodged: you can work less, buy less, or score low on purpose.

    erase dodge

Britain tried the flat charge (@draw poll_box poll_line_1 poll_line_2@) per adult, from 1990: the poll tax, with discounts for the poorest.

B: Lump-sum, and close  (@highlight poll_line_2 effect underline@)to useless for fairness, since the duke and the cleaner paid much the same (@draw poll_line_3@). It set off riots and was gone by 1993.

Height is  (@draw height_box height_line_1@)the odd one. Tall people earn more on average, and nobody grows taller to dodge a tax.

B: Greg Mankiw and Matthew Weinzierl ran the standard theory on  (@draw height_line_2@)it: a tall person earning fifty thousand dollars should pay a (@draw height_line_3 big@)bout four and a half thousand more than a short one.

Almost nobody wants that tax. Their point: either we tax height, or our idea of fairness isn't only about outcomes.
    highlight big effect circle

    pause 0.5

## How sure, and the test
use: ppf
with:
    good_x: Ada's well-being
    good_y: Jon's well-being
    x_max: 10
    y_max: 10
    shade_attainable: true
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 60
      w: 620
      h: 600
    points:
      - {x_share: 0.18, label: Market}
      - {x_share: 0.55, label: Fair}
      - {x_share: 0.55, label: 'Fair, real taxes', kind: inefficient}

    text c1 "Theorem: consensus" #2f6b8f x 815 y 520 font_size 26 hidden true
    text c2 "Can't tax ability: mostly agreed" #3d3833 x 815 y 450 font_size 24 hidden true
    text c3 "Cost of fairness: contested" #b5482e x 815 y 380 font_size 24 hidden true
    text onp "On paper: Fair" #2f6b8f x 800 y 480 font_size 30 hidden true
    text inp "In practice: inside the curve" #b5482e x 800 y 400 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "How sure are we?"

So the theorem's transfer can't be built. Where does that leave a real town that wants the fair point?
    draw axes frontier attainable pt_0 pt_label_0

B: It can still tax catch and income, and mov (@draw pt_1 pt_label_1 pt_2@)e crates to Ada. But where does that land?

    ask "Harbourtown pays Ada with a tax on each crate. Does it reach Fair, or the point inside the curve? Click one." choose ["pt_1","pt_2"] answer pt_2
        right: Inside the curve: the tax costs some catch on the way.
        wrong: A tax per crate makes Jon fish less, so some catch is lost: here, the point inside the curve.

Inside the curve. T (@draw pt_label_2@)he fair split arrives, but with less fish than the market could have caught.

B: Every step toward fairness now costs some efficiency, and real tax policy is about how much of that cost to accept.
    point at.ref pt_2 gesture circle

How sure are we? The theore (@draw c1@)m is a proof, so that part is consensus.

B: That nobod (@draw c2@)y can tax ability lump-sum is mostly agreed. Some see a little room in tags like height.

How big the leak is, how much catch fairness (@draw c3@) really costs, is contested, and the evidence is still coming in.

    erase c1 c2 c3

    quiz "A country pays every adult $1,000 a year, no conditions, and funds it with an income tax. Which part changes what an extra hour of work pays?" intro "Two new cases." voice b
        right: The income tax: it takes a slice of every extra hour's pay. The flat $1,000 is the same whatever you do.
        wrong: Ask which part grows or shrinks when you work one more hour: here, the income tax.
        * The $1,000 payment
        + The income tax
        * Neither
        * Both

    quiz "Two towns raise the same money: one with a flat fee per household, one with a 30% tax on earnings. Where do people work more?"
        right: With the flat fee: an extra hour keeps its full pay, so nobody gains by working less.
        wrong: Which one makes an extra hour pay less? The 30% tax does, so here: with the flat fee.
        + With the flat fee
        * With the 30% tax
        * The same in both

So can markets deliver any fair outcome we want? On pa (@draw onp@)per, yes, after a transfer nobody can design. In practice fairne (@draw inp@)ss has a price, and someone has to decide how much it is worth.

    pause 0.6

